    "checks": [
     "Consistent with the order of treatment in A&S (Units 7, 13, 23)"
    ]
   },
   "part": "p5"
  },
  {
   "id": "ii7-inversions",
   "chapter": "ch14",
   "title": "ii7 and its inversions",
   "kind": "table",
   "summary": "The supertonic seventh is a predominant chord leading to V. ii6/5 is the most common position; it sounds like IV with an added 6th.",
   "rules": [
    "Resolve the chordal 7th down: 1̂ → 7̂.",
    "An incomplete ii7 must keep the root and the 7th: omit the 5th of a m7 (or M7) chord, the 3rd of a ø7 (or °7) chord.",
    "Never double the chordal 7th or the leading tone; double the root."
   ],
   "guidelines": [
    "Approach the chordal 7th as a suspension (common tone) or passing tone — far more common than neighbour or appoggiatura approaches.",
    "The 7th may be held over a cadential 6/4 and resolve when V arrives."
   ],
